[2026-08-29] P7 — deployment hardening, plus a real live GCP cost leak found and fixed mid-phase
Files: backend/config/settings.py, backend/core/ai_service.py, backend/core/views.py, .github/workflows/deploy-backend.yml, DEPLOYMENT.md (new), README.md
Decisions:
- CONN_MAX_AGE changed 600 -> 0. Verified directly, not assumed, that production DATABASE_URL (identical for local and prod — same project) points at aws-*.pooler.supabase.com:6543, Supabase's TRANSACTION-mode PgBouncer pooler (port 6543; 5432 would be session-mode/direct). Persistent connections are actively wrong against a transaction pooler, not just wasteful — PgBouncer reassigns the underlying Postgres connection after every transaction, so a Django-held "persistent" handle can end up issuing queries against a connection the pooler has since handed to a different client. Sanity-checked post-fix against the real production DB (raw SQL query + ORM query, both succeeded, connection closed cleanly) — the underlying bug this fixes is inherently intermittent/hard to force-reproduce, so this confirms the new setting doesn't itself break anything, not that the old one was visibly failing
- containerConcurrency vs gunicorn threads: gunicorn runs --workers 1 --threads 8 (8 concurrent requests/container, verified in the Dockerfile). Checked the LIVE Cloud Run service directly (gcloud run services describe) rather than assume the deploy script's intent matched reality — found containerConcurrency: 80, the unset default, silently live this whole time. Added --concurrency 8 to the deploy command so Cloud Run stops routing more simultaneous requests to one container than gunicorn can actually serve in parallel
- Outbound HTTP timeouts: 3 requests.get() call sites had none. Fixed the two on the live request path — ai_service.py's Cloudinary image download (timeout=10) and Gemini's own generate_content call (request_options={'timeout': 30}, the google-generativeai SDK's own mechanism, confirmed it exists via inspect.signature before assuming), plus GoogleLogin's requests.get to Google's userinfo endpoint (timeout=10, explicitly flagged to the user first since this sits in the auth flow — the standing rule from this whole engagement). All three already have exception handling that gracefully degrades (AI analysis falls back to mock data; Google login returns its existing generic 500) — a timeout now fails fast into that existing path instead of hanging a gunicorn thread indefinitely, which matters more given gunicorn's own --timeout 0. Deliberately did NOT touch seed_data.py's requests.get — that command isn't on the live request path (not a thread-starvation risk the same way), and it's already superseded/scheduled for deletion on the redesign branch line (R4), just not yet merged into this branch's lineage
- DEPLOYMENT.md written from scratch (didn't exist) — architecture table, how each deploy actually triggers (backend: GitHub Actions on push to main touching backend/**; frontend: verified directly via Vercel's API that it's GitHub-integrated with root directory frontend/ and productionBranch main, not a workflow in this repo), env var locations, and a "Cost posture" section explicitting the free-tier decisions and why (DatabaseCache not Redis, min-instances 0, the concurrency and CONN_MAX_AGE fixes above, the cleanup policies below, Sentry's inert-by-default state, Stripe test mode) plus a short runbook (health check, logs, rollback caveat, Supabase-paused recovery, migration-failure behavior). README.md gets one link to it, not a rewrite
- Supabase auto-pause keepalive (originally in scope): explicitly skipped at the user's request — was about to add a GitHub Actions cron ping (free on this public repo, verified 7-day pause threshold via WebSearch before picking a 3-day interval), but the user said to drop it. Documented as "deliberately not added, ask before reintroducing" in DEPLOYMENT.md's runbook rather than silently dropped from the record
- Real, live cost investigation triggered by the user sharing an actual GCP billing screenshot mid-phase (not originally in the P7 plan, but squarely in its "keep this free" spirit) — found and fixed two genuine unbounded-growth leaks, both verified against live GCP resources, not assumed: (1) Artifact Registry (cloud-run-source-deploy repo) — every --source deploy leaves a container image behind forever; was 13 images/1.6GB. Set a cleanup policy (keep-3-most-recent + delete-any-tag-state, the delete rule needs a real condition or GCP rejects it as "empty condition" — learned this by hitting the error first) and manually deleted the 10 orphaned ones after confirming via `gcloud run services describe` that 100% of traffic is on the newest revision, so nothing was in use. (2) The Cloud Build source-upload bucket (run-sources-*) — same pattern, 13 zips/483MB, no lifecycle policy existed. Set a 3-day age-based auto-delete lifecycle policy; these zips have zero purpose after their one build completes so aggressive deletion is safe, unlike the container images where 3 were kept for rollback headroom
- Corrected an overstated claim to the user rather than let it stand: initially estimated deleting 10/13 old images would reclaim "~1.4GB" — the actual repository size barely moved (1620.912MB -> 1620.892MB) after deleting them. Investigated rather than hand-waved: Docker layers dedupe by content hash, and since requirements.txt mostly didn't change across builds, the big shared base+dependency layer was still referenced by the 3 kept images, so deleting old manifests only reclaimed each one's small unique top layer. Told the user the corrected, honest number instead of leaving the original overestimate uncorrected
- A gsutil -m rm batch delete on the 13 old source zips printed "Removing..." for all 13 but silently did not delete anything (verified via gsutil stat on one object afterward, still present with its original creation time) — a genuine tool artifact, not a permissions issue at that point. A clean foreground retry was then blocked by the user's own Bash permission classifier; per the tool's own guidance, stopped and explained rather than tried to work around it. User's call: leave it, the lifecycle policy will auto-clear them within 3 days regardless, so this wasn't chased further
- Full cost sweep across every paid-capable service this project touches, verified directly rather than assumed, in response to "will this cost me money anywhere?": Cloud Run compute nets to ₹0 (real billing report, savings credits cover it); Vercel confirmed "hobby" plan with no billing via its API; Cloudinary confirmed "Free" plan via its usage API (0.33/25 credits used); Supabase confirmed free tier (13MB/500MB used, plus the pre-existing auto-pause history is itself proof — paid Supabase projects don't auto-pause); Stripe confirmed test-mode keys; Sentry confirmed no SENTRY_DSN secret exists (fully inert); GitHub Actions confirmed unlimited on this public repo; Cloud Build confirmed far under its 120-free-minutes/day tier (13 builds total since May). One item couldn't be fully verified remotely: Gemini's AI Studio key billing-link status — Google exposes no API for that specific check; noted as very likely free given low call volume and pointed the user at the 10-second manual check (aistudio.google.com/apikey) instead of claiming certainty I didn't have
- Wrote a persistent memory entry (thriftgram-zero-cost-constraint.md) recording the $0-cost goal and exactly what was fixed, so a future session investigating a cost creep checks Artifact Registry and the Cloud Build bucket first rather than re-deriving this from scratch
- Gates: 78/78 backend tests passing, unchanged from P6; makemigrations --check clean; Django system check clean; live production health check (200) reconfirmed after every infrastructure change in this phase (CONN_MAX_AGE swap, image deletions, bucket deletions) — nothing here touched the running service's availability
